window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] = window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"] || {};
window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-3-full-book"][4] = "<p>Most product problems have one of a few shapes, and a stock structure gives you a sensible first cut so that your attention goes to the content. The library in FW-C03-06 holds six: the <em>funnel</em> (each step of a user path), <em>supply and demand</em> (buyers, sellers and the match between them), <em>segments</em> (new and returning, platform, region), <em>time</em> (before and after, step or trend, seasonality), <em>ecosystem</em> (partners, competitors, regulators) and <em>inputs</em> (volume × rate × value). Choose by asking which cut best serves the decision, say the structure aloud, and expect to use two: one to split the problem and a second to examine the branch you picked. A stock structure is a starting point. It misleads when the problem does not have its shape, such as treating a policy change as a funnel problem, and it can pull you toward the questions the structure makes easy. C13 applies several of these structures to metric changes.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-8-symptoms-versus-causes-and-the-root-cause-methods-that\"></span>\n<h4>C03.8 Symptoms versus causes, and the root-cause methods that survive scrutiny</h4>\n<p>A <strong>symptom</strong> is an observable sign of a problem, and a <strong>root cause</strong> is the underlying condition that, if removed, stops the problem from recurring. <a href=\"#gl-root-cause\">root cause</a> separates the two. &quot;Conversion fell&quot; is a symptom. &quot;A payment-library update broke one checkout step on older Android versions&quot; is a cause.</p>\n<p><strong>The symptom test:</strong> if I fix this, does the problem stop recurring, or only this instance? A fix that handles only this instance treats a symptom.</p>\n<p><strong>Three layers.</strong> Distinguish the proximate cause (the immediate trigger), the contributing causes (conditions that let it happen or made it worse), and the root cause (the condition whose removal prevents recurrence). Most real failures have more than one contributing cause <code>Inferred</code>, and hunting for a single root cause can mislead. Ask which conditions, if changed, would have prevented the failure.</p>\n<p><strong>&quot;Human error&quot; is rarely a root cause.</strong> If a person made an error, the productive question is what made that error easy to make and hard to catch.</p>\n<p><strong>The five whys.</strong> The <strong>five whys</strong> is a technique that asks &quot;why&quot; repeatedly, usually about five times, until it reaches a cause that can be acted on. <a href=\"#gl-five-whys\">five whys</a> is simple and useful for linear causes. Its weaknesses are that it follows one chain, it stops where the questioner stops, and it can end at a person. Use it with a second method for branching failures (FW-C03-08).</p>\n<p><strong>Mitigate, then diagnose.</strong> When harm is ongoing and the cause is unknown, the first job is to limit the harm with an action you can reverse and whose effects you understand, such as pausing a rollout with a <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a>. The second is to find the cause. The mistake is acting on a guess that changes the system in ways you cannot predict. The case in CS-C03-02 shows what happens when a fix is attempted before the cause is understood <code>[V: SEC administrative order on Knight Capital]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>When fixing a symptom is right.</strong> If the root cause is outside your control (a carrier's delays), or its removal costs more than the harm, treating the symptom is a legitimate choice. Say you are doing it and what you give up.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Counterexample.</strong> A support team keeps resetting a customer's account when it locks. Each reset fixes the instance, and the lock recurs weekly. The pattern is the signal: a recurring instance-level fix means the cause was never found. Conversely, a team that insists on finding the root cause of a one-time outage that cost nothing and cannot recur spends effort for no gain. Scale the depth of the search to the cost of recurrence.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c03-3-frameworks\"></span>\n<h3>3. Frameworks</h3>\n<p>Eight frameworks, set out below. FW-C03-01 to FW-C03-04 are the core sequence for an analytical answer; FW-C03-05 is the loop for building; FW-C03-06 is the library of reusable structures (index section C03.5); FW-C03-07 is the time-pressure skeleton (C03.6, detailed in section 4); FW-C03-08 covers root-cause methods (C03.8).</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-01\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-01 · The frame card · Original</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a vague prompt; the interviewer's or stakeholder's answers to three to five clarifying questions.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a half-page statement of the decision, the question, definitions, scope, success criterion, constraints and assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> someone can answer clarifying questions, or you can state reasonable assumptions and flag them.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> time spent framing versus time spent analyzing; a frame that is too tight can exclude the real cause.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> asking many questions and never framing; framing the question around a favored solution; leaving the decision unstated so every branch looks equally useful.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> &quot;Ratings dropped&quot; becomes: decision (what to fix first), question (why did the average rating fall from 4.6 to 4.3 over two months), definitions (rating is the post-delivery star score), scope (all cities, all orders), success (identify the top cause with enough confidence to act), constraints (a day of analyst time). See the Baseline, Strong and Standout answers in section 6.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Prompt</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Decision</td>\n<td>What will someone do differently depending on the answer?</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Question</td>\n<td>The prompt restated as a specific question</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Definitions</td>\n<td>How each term is measured</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Scope</td>\n<td>Segments, time window, platforms, regions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Success</td>\n<td>What would count as a good enough answer</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constraints</td>\n<td>Time, data, people</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Assumptions</td>\n<td>What you are taking as true until told otherwise</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-02\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-02 · The MECE cut tests · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a proposed split of a problem into branches.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a corrected split plus a note on what is left out on purpose.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the whole is well defined; the cut matters for the decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> rigor versus speed; perfect exclusivity is rarely achievable and can cost time that is better spent testing.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> passing the overlap test but not the decision test (a clean cut that leads to the same action in every branch); hiding a large unexamined area in &quot;other&quot;; siblings at different levels of abstraction.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> &quot;mobile, iOS, new users&quot; fails the overlap test (iOS is mobile) and the gap test (web and returning users are missing); replaced with &quot;iOS, Android, web,&quot; then a second tree by new versus returning users if the decision needs it.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Five tests, in order:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Overlap test.</strong> Can any item fit in two branches? If yes, redraw.</li>\n<li><strong>Gap test.</strong> Is there a plausible item that fits no branch? If yes, add a branch or declare the exclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Decision test.</strong> Would different branches lead to different actions? If not, change the cut.</li>\n<li><strong>Size test.</strong> Is &quot;other&quot; more than about a fifth of the whole? If so, find the missing branch.</li>\n<li><strong>Level test.</strong> Are siblings at the same level of abstraction? (&quot;Price&quot; and &quot;iOS 17 users&quot; are not siblings.)</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-03\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-03 · The scored issue tree · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a root question, a chosen cut, and whatever facts you already have.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a tree two to three levels deep, each leaf scored for likelihood, impact if true and cost to check, and a short ordered list of what to check first.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> scoring can be rough (low, medium, high); some checks are cheap enough to do early.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> careful scoring takes time; early facts may change the scores.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> scoring that justifies the branch you already like; treating every leaf as equally important; checking the easy leaf because it is easy rather than because it is likely (the streetlight effect, section 9).</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the onboarding funnel tree in Worked example 1 scores five step branches, finds one step holds a quarter of all installs lost, and checks it first.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Branch or leaf</th>\n<th>Likelihood</th>\n<th>Impact if true</th>\n<th>Cost to check</th>\n<th>Check order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>(name)</td>\n<td>L / M / H</td>\n<td>L / M / H</td>\n<td>L / M / H</td>\n<td>1, 2, 3...</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rule for ordering:</strong> high likelihood and low cost first; high likelihood and high cost second; low likelihood and low cost third; low likelihood and high cost last, or drop. Medium-likelihood leaves sit between the second and third groups, ordered by impact.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-04\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-04 · The hypothesis card · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a leaf from the scored tree, plus the context.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a card stating the claim, mechanism, predictions, the test, the rejection condition, the action if true and the cost to check.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the claim is specific enough to be false; the test data exists or can be obtained.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> writing cards slows the first minutes; it speeds the rest by preventing vague checks.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> a claim that cannot fail; a test that could not tell the hypothesis from its rivals; an action field left blank.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> &quot;The drop is concentrated in Android on version 5.2&quot; with predictions for iOS and for dates, a rejection rule, and the action (roll back the flag).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Field</th>\n<th>Content</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Claim</td>\n<td>One specific sentence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Because</td>\n<td>The mechanism</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>We would also expect</td>\n<td>Predictions that would be true if the claim is true</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>The check and the data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reject if</td>\n<td>The result that would rule it out</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>If true, we will</td>\n<td>The action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Cost to check</td>\n<td>Time and effort</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-05\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-05 · The Build–Measure–Learn loop · Established</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> Eric Ries, <em>The Lean Startup</em> (2011).</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a risky assumption (what must be true for the idea to work) and the smallest test of it.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a measured result against a criterion set in advance, and a decision to continue, change course or stop.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the assumption can be tested with real users or data quickly; the team will act on the result.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> speed of learning versus polish of what is shown to users; a small test can mislead if it is unrepresentative.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> building without a stated criterion; measuring what is easy rather than what tests the assumption; changing the criterion after the result; a loop too slow to inform decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> to test whether a weekly digest email raises retention, build a manual version for 500 users, measure 30-day return against a pre-set threshold, and decide on automation only if the threshold is met.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: (1) name the assumption that, if false, makes the idea fail; (2) design the smallest build that tests it; (3) write the success criterion and decision rule before you run it; (4) run and measure; (5) learn, and decide: continue, change, or stop. The speed of the loop often matters more than the polish of any one pass: a team that completes ten loops learns more than a team that completes two excellent ones, provided each loop tests a real assumption.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-06\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-06 · The structure library · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a problem type and the decision it serves.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a recommended starting cut, with the first leaf checks and the situations where it misleads.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> problems repeat in shape; a standard structure is a starting point and may be combined or replaced.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> speed versus fit; a stock structure can pull you toward the questions it makes easy.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> using a favorite structure for every problem; combining structures that overlap; skipping the decision test.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> for &quot;marketplace conversion fell,&quot; the supply-and-demand structure separates a shortage of listings from a drop in buyer intent, and a time structure then checks whether the change was a step or a trend (Worked example 2).</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Structure</th>\n<th>Root split</th>\n<th>Use when</th>\n<th>Typical first checks</th>\n<th>Misleads when</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Funnel</td>\n<td>Each step in a sequence</td>\n<td>A user path leads to an outcome</td>\n<td>Step conversion and drop-off by step</td>\n<td>Users skip steps or re-enter</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Supply and demand</td>\n<td>Buyers, sellers, matching</td>\n<td>A two-sided or capacity-limited system</td>\n<td>Requests, available supply, match rate</td>\n<td>A third party (regulator, platform) is the driver</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Segments</td>\n<td>New and returning; platform; region; source</td>\n<td>You suspect the change is not uniform</td>\n<td>The metric by segment</td>\n<td>Segments overlap or mix shifts (see <a href=\"#gl-mix-shift\">mix shift</a>, C13)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>Before and after; trend and step; seasonality</td>\n<td>The change has a date</td>\n<td>The metric plotted against launches and events</td>\n<td>The timeline is confounded by several simultaneous changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ecosystem</td>\n<td>The product, its partners, competitors, regulators</td>\n<td>An external party could be the cause</td>\n<td>Competitor moves, partner changes, policy</td>\n<td>You assume outside causes before checking your own</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Inputs</td>\n<td>Volume × rate × value</td>\n<td>The metric is a product of factors</td>\n<td>Which factor moved</td>\n<td>The factors are not independent</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Combining.</strong> Most real problems need two: one for the cut that serves the decision and one for a second pass on the branch you picked. Name each structure aloud and say why.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-07\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-07 · The time-pressure skeleton · Original</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a prompt, ten minutes (or the portion of a 45-minute round you have), and a pen.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a spoken or written answer with a frame, a structure, a priority and a conclusion.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the interviewer will answer clarifying questions and may redirect.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> structure first costs the first minutes; it saves the middle and protects the end.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> diving deep into the first branch; never reaching a conclusion; ignoring the interviewer's redirection.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> the AI-assistant case in Worked example 4, with timings.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Steps: <strong>Clarify and frame, Structure, Prioritize, Test, Conclude.</strong> Detailed with time budgets in section 4.</p>\n<span id=\"fw-c03-08\"></span>\n<h5>FW-C03-08 · The root-cause toolkit · Established</h5>\n<p><strong>Core framework.</strong> Learn this one first.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Origin:</strong> the five whys from Taiichi Ohno's account of the Toyota Production System (English edition 1988); the fishbone (cause-and-effect) diagram from Kaoru Ishikawa's quality-control work in Japan, commonly dated to the 1960s (some sources place its first use earlier); fault tree analysis developed at Bell Telephone Laboratories in the early 1960s, by H. A. Watson, for a US Air Force study of the Minuteman launch control system <code>[V: fault tree origin from an encyclopedia entry; Ishikawa dating varies by source; Ohno's account of the five whys is listed in the reading list]</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> a failure or recurring problem, and what is known about its timeline.</li>\n<li><strong>Outputs:</strong> a causal explanation naming proximate cause, contributing causes and a root cause, with corrective actions.</li>\n<li><strong>Assumptions:</strong> the failure can be traced; the team will look at process, not blame.</li>\n<li><strong>Tradeoffs:</strong> depth versus time; the techniques are cheap and the discipline to apply them is the cost.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure modes:</strong> five whys that stops at &quot;human error&quot;; a fishbone used as a brainstorm with no testing; a fault tree drawn from memory without evidence; multiple causes collapsed into one.</li>\n<li><strong>Worked example:</strong> a notification outage analyzed by the five whys (a linear chain) and a fault tree (an AND of two conditions), shown below.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Which methods survive scrutiny in product work.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Method</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n<th>Strength</th>\n<th>Weakness</th>\n<th>Verdict</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Five whys</td>\n<td>Linear causes in a process</td>\n<td>Fast; builds the habit of asking why</td>\n<td>One chain; stops where you stop</td>\n<td>Keep, with discipline</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fishbone diagram</td>\n<td>Brainstorming contributing causes by category</td>\n<td>Prompts breadth</td>\n<td>Lists possibilities without testing them</td>\n<td>Use as a prompt, not an analysis</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fault tree</td>\n<td>Failures that need several conditions together (AND) or any of several (OR)</td>\n<td>Shows logical structure and single points of failure</td>\n<td>Time-consuming; needs evidence</td>\n<td>Keep for launch and incident work</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>MECE issue tree</td>\n<td>Diagnosis before the cause is known</td>\n<td>Ties to the decision</td>\n<td>Depends on the cut</td>\n<td>The default for interviews</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>A short example.</strong> A push-notification feature sent no notifications for six hours. Five whys: (1) Why did users receive none? The send job did not run. (2) Why? The scheduler's credentials had expired. (3) Why? The renewal reminder went to a former employee's mailbox. (4) Why?</p>\n<p>Ownership of the credential was never reassigned. (5) Why? No checklist covers offboarding for service credentials. Cause: a process gap, not a person. A fault tree for the same outage shows an AND: the outage required both an expired credential and no monitoring alert on the send volume. Removing either condition would have prevented or shortened it, so the corrective actions are an ownership check and a send-volume alert.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-4-answer-architecture\"></span>\n<h3>4. Answer architecture</h3>\n<p>Three question types use this chapter directly. The skeleton in FW-C03-07 is the same for all three; what changes is the root question and the cut.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-6-structuring-under-time-pressure-the-skeleton-the-budge\"></span>\n<h4>C03.6 Structuring under time pressure: the skeleton, the budget and the signposts</h4>\n<p><strong>The skeleton: Clarify and frame, Structure, Prioritize, Test, Conclude.</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Clarify and frame</strong> (about 20% of the time). Ask the three to five questions that could change your structure. State the frame in two sentences: the decision, the question, the scope and the assumptions.</li>\n<li><strong>Structure</strong> (about 20%). Name the cut and say why it serves the decision. Lay out three to five branches. Say what you are excluding on purpose.</li>\n<li><strong>Prioritize</strong> (about 15%). Pick the two or three branches you will examine first, and say why: likelihood, impact, cost to check.</li>\n<li><strong>Test</strong> (about 35%). For each chosen branch, state a hypothesis, the check, and what you would conclude. If the interviewer gives data, update aloud: which hypothesis it lowers and which it raises.</li>\n<li><strong>Conclude</strong> (about 10%). State what you believe, how confident you are, and the next action. If it is incomplete, say what you would do next and what would change your view.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Time budget for a ten-minute segment</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Minutes</th>\n<th>What to produce</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarify and frame</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>Three to five questions; a two-sentence frame</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Structure</td>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A named cut, three to five branches, stated exclusions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prioritize</td>\n<td>1.5</td>\n<td>Two or three branches chosen, with reasons</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Test</td>\n<td>3.5</td>\n<td>Hypothesis, check and conclusion per chosen branch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Conclude</td>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Belief, confidence, next action</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>In a 45-minute round you may run this skeleton once at length or two to three times on short sub-problems, and the interviewer will steer. Treat their redirection as data, not interruption.</p>\n<p><strong>Signposts that keep you and the interviewer oriented</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Let me take a moment to structure this.&quot; (Thirty to sixty seconds of silence is acceptable and better than rambling.)</li>\n<li>&quot;I'll cut this by funnel step because the decision is which step to fix.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;That gives four branches. I'll look at two first, because they are the most likely and the cheapest to check.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;That rules out X because Y. That raises Z.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;To summarize: I believe it's A; I'm moderately confident; the next step is B; I'd change my view if C.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Pacing rules.</strong> Go broad before deep: set the top-level branches before descending into any one. Spend depth on the branches you prioritized. Check in at natural breaks (&quot;Shall I go deeper on supply or move to demand?&quot;) without asking permission for every step. If you are stuck, back up one level and ask what other cut would serve the decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-type-a-the-open-ended-diagnostic-why-did-x-happen\"></span>\n<h4>Type A: the open-ended diagnostic (&quot;Why did X happen?&quot;)</h4>\n<p>Root question: &quot;Why did [metric] change?&quot; Frame the metric, magnitude, timing and scope; choose a cut by what you can act on (funnel, supply and demand, segments or time, FW-C03-06); build a scored tree; test the top branches with hypothesis cards; conclude with the cause you believe, your confidence, and the action. C13 adds the product-metric diagnostic sequence in full detail, including data checks and a taxonomy of causes.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-type-b-the-decision-question-should-we-do-x\"></span>\n<h4>Type B: the decision question (&quot;Should we do X?&quot;)</h4>\n<p>Root question: &quot;Should we do X?&quot; A decision tree splits into four questions that are exclusive in purpose and together cover most product decisions:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Value:</strong> is the need real and how big (who, how many, how painful, how much revenue or retention)?</li>\n<li><strong>Feasibility:</strong> can we build and run it (effort, dependencies, risk)?</li>\n<li><strong>Viability:</strong> does it work for the business (cost, pricing, strategic fit)?</li>\n<li><strong>Risk and reversibility:</strong> what could go wrong, and can we undo it (FW-C02-04)?</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Evaluate each with evidence you have and evidence you lack, then recommend with the cost stated (FW-C02-03). The structure echoes the value, feasibility and viability lens familiar from design practice, with risk and reversibility added because C02 treats them as deciding variables.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-type-c-structure-this-problem-with-no-data\"></span>\n<h4>Type C: &quot;Structure this problem&quot; with no data</h4>\n<p><em>Example prompt:</em> &quot;How would you think about improving engagement on a music app?&quot; The skeleton is the same, with two differences: define the term first (&quot;engagement&quot; is not a metric until you choose one), and give the cut and the priority, not a conclusion. A good answer ends with a structured plan: the chosen metric, the branches, the two you would examine first, and what each check would tell you.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-5-worked-examples\"></span>\n<h3>5. Worked examples</h3>\n<p>All four examples are <code>Illustrative</code>: the products are fictional and every number is invented. Each one is chosen to show a different structure from FW-C03-06 and a different decision.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-worked-example-1-consumer-subscription-why-do-first-lessons-\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 1 — Consumer subscription: why do first lessons go unfinished? (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A language-learning app finds that only 38% of installs complete a first lesson (invented). &quot;Why aren't people finishing?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and frame.</strong> Questions asked: how is &quot;completed first lesson&quot; defined (a lesson reaching the final screen), how long has the figure been at this level (stable for three months), and does it differ by acquisition source. Answers (invented): ad-driven installs complete at 28% and organic installs at 51%, and 56% of installs are ad-driven. Check: 0.56 × 28% + 0.44 × 51% = 15.7% + 22.4% = 38.1%, which matches the blended figure, so the segment data and the headline agree.</p>\n<p><em>Frame card:</em> decision, which part of the first-session experience to fix first; question, where and why installs stop before finishing a lesson; scope, all new installs, last 90 days; success, the one or two steps that explain most of the loss; constraint, one week of analyst time.</p>\n<p><strong>Choose the cut.</strong> The decision is which step to fix, so a funnel cut serves it (FW-C03-06). Each install leaves at exactly one step, which makes the branches exclusive.</p>\n<p><strong>Build and score the tree.</strong> Root: &quot;Why do 62% of installs not finish a first lesson?&quot; Step conversion data (invented), with the product of the steps matching the headline:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Step</th>\n<th>Conversion from previous step</th>\n<th>Of 100 installs, still present</th>\n<th>Lost at this step</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Install to first open</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>90.0</td>\n<td>10.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Open to account created</td>\n<td>72%</td>\n<td>64.8</td>\n<td>25.2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Account to goal setup</td>\n<td>85%</td>\n<td>55.1</td>\n<td>9.7</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Setup to lesson started</td>\n<td>80%</td>\n<td>44.1</td>\n<td>11.0</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lesson started to finished</td>\n<td>86%</td>\n<td>37.9</td>\n<td>6.2</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Check: 0.90 × 0.72 × 0.85 × 0.80 × 0.86 = 0.379, which matches 38%. Of the 62 installs lost per 100, 25 are lost at account creation, the largest single loss.</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Branch</th>\n<th>Likelihood</th>\n<th>Impact if true</th>\n<th>Cost to check</th>\n<th>Order</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Account creation step</td>\n<td>High (largest loss)</td>\n<td>High (25 of 62)</td>\n<td>Low (data in hand)</td>\n<td>1</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Source mix (ad versus organic)</td>\n<td>High (gap of 23 points)</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>2</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Setup to lesson started</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium (11 of 62)</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>3</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Install to first open</td>\n<td>Medium</td>\n<td>Medium (10 of 62)</td>\n<td>Medium (needs store data)</td>\n<td>4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Instrumentation error</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>High</td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>check once, early</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses for branch 1.</strong> (H1) Forcing account creation before any value is shown loses casual users. (H2) Email verification adds friction. (H3) Permission prompts shown on the same screen cause abandonment. Tests: compare drop-off by sign-up method (social login versus email); measure time on the screen; watch ten session recordings. Rejection: if social-login users drop at the same rate as email users, H2 is rejected; if the drop is the same for installs that saw no permission prompt, H3 is rejected.</p>\n<p><strong>Second look at source mix.</strong> The ad-versus-organic gap says either the account step is worse for ad-driven users (intent mismatch) or they differ elsewhere. Splitting the account-step conversion by source separates them.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclude.</strong> &quot;I believe the account step is the main cause: it loses a quarter of installs, and the gap between sources suggests ad-driven users arrive with less intent. My confidence is medium until I see the split by sign-up method. The reversible action is to test trying a first lesson before sign-up behind a <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a> for 20% of new installs, with 30-day <a href=\"#gl-retention\">retention</a> as a <a href=\"#gl-guardrail-metric\">guardrail metric</a> so that we do not trade completion for later churn.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The decision chose the cut (funnel). Arithmetic on the data checked that the tree was complete. Scoring ordered the work. Two structures were combined (funnel, then segments).</p>\n<span id=\"c03-worked-example-2-marketplace-wait-times-doubled-illustrative\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 2 — Marketplace: wait times doubled (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> In one city of a ride-hailing app, median rider wait time rose from 4 to 8 minutes over six weeks (invented). &quot;What's going on?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Clarify and frame.</strong> Definition: wait is from request to driver arrival. Gradual over six weeks, not a step. One city, not others. Nothing launched in the dispatch system in that window, according to the team (invented).</p>\n<p><strong>Choose the cut.</strong> A wait is an outcome of three things: how many riders ask (demand), how many drivers are available (supply), and how well the two are matched (matching). That is a supply-and-demand structure. Add a measurement branch because a changed definition would look identical.</p>\n<p><em>Tested for MECE:</em> demand, supply, matching and measurement together cover the ways the number can move. The draft &quot;bad weather, drivers, app bugs&quot; failed the tests: weather and bugs describe causes, drivers describes an actor, and the three do not share a level.</p>\n<p><strong>Priorities.</strong> The change was gradual, so a recent release is less likely and slow-moving supply or demand shifts are more likely. All four checks are cheap; run measurement first as a one-minute check, then the others in parallel.</p>\n<p><strong>Data from the interviewer (invented).</strong> Requests per day rose 5%. Online driver-hours fell 18%. So requests per online driver-hour rose by about 28% (1.05 divided by 0.82 is 1.28). Wait times in a capacity-limited system rise faster than the load does when utilization is high, so a 28% rise in load per driver can double waits. The magnitude is plausible, and the check is whether pickup distances rose as expected.</p>\n<p><strong>Next level under supply.</strong> Online driver-hours = active drivers × hours per active driver. Data (invented): active drivers fell 6% and hours per active driver fell 13%, and 0.94 × 0.87 = 0.82, matching the 18% fall. Most of the fall is in hours per driver, which is a behavior change, not attrition.</p>\n<p><strong>Hypotheses.</strong> (H1) Earnings per online hour fell, so drivers work less. (H2) A competitor raised incentives and drivers are splitting hours. (H3) A recent change to the app's incentive rules reduced guaranteed earnings. Checks: earnings per online hour by week; driver survey; incentive calendar. Data (invented): earnings per online hour fell 11% in week two, when an incentive rule was narrowed.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclude.</strong> &quot;Supply of driver hours is the cause, and the likely trigger is the incentive change in week two. I'm fairly confident, because the timing, the size and the mechanism line up. Mitigation: reinstate targeted incentives in the affected hours, which is reversible, and track cost per ride as a <a href=\"#gl-counter-metric\">counter metric</a>. Then confirm with a survey whether the competitor hypothesis also contributes.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The cut came from the system's equation. Arithmetic on the data confirmed that the sub-branches add up. The decision was reversible and had a counter-metric.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-worked-example-3-b2b-a-decision-tree-for-should-we-build-the\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 3 — B2B: a decision tree for &quot;should we build the integration?&quot; (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> A sales-analytics product is asked to build an integration with a popular customer-relationship system. &quot;Should we?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Frame.</strong> The decision is build now, build later, partner or decline. Success is a choice we can defend with evidence and a stated cost.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision tree.</strong> Root question: &quot;Should we build it?&quot; Four branches (Type B structure):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Value.</strong> Of the last 40 lost deals, 12 cited the missing integration (invented), worth about $30,000 a year each, or $360,000 in annual revenue (invented). Caution: lost-deal reasons are what buyers say, and the <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a> (C06) applies; check against deals won without the integration and against what support hears.</li>\n<li><strong>Feasibility.</strong> Twelve engineer-weeks to build and half an engineer ongoing (invented). The partner's programming interface is documented but rate-limited.</li>\n<li><strong>Viability.</strong> The partner's terms allow listing in their marketplace for a fee (invented). Dependency risk: the partner can change terms or compete.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk and reversibility.</strong> Building is partly reversible (the code can be retired, but customers who adopt it will expect it to remain). Classify with FW-C02-04: error cost medium, waiting cost moderate, reversibility partial, so stage it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Build the full integration</td>\n<td>Win rate in lost-deal segment</td>\n<td>Twelve engineer-weeks, ongoing load, dependency</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Build a thin version for the top use case with five design partners</td>\n<td>Evidence before commitment</td>\n<td>Slower to market; partial coverage</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Use a third-party connector tool</td>\n<td>Speed</td>\n<td>Margin and dependence on a third party</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Decline</td>\n<td>Engineering capacity</td>\n<td>Continued losses in the segment</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Recommendation.</strong> &quot;I recommend the thin version with five design partners, time-boxed to six weeks. It gives up full coverage now to avoid committing twelve engineer-weeks on evidence that is partly self-reported. It is reversible in the sense that it can be retired before general launch. I would expand if at least three of the five partners use it weekly and the sales team shows a measurable change in the segment's win rate, and I would stop if partner terms change.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> A decision tree, unlike a diagnostic tree, asks whether to act. The branches are different questions (value, feasibility, viability, risk) rather than possible causes. The C02 tools (reversibility, cost of delay) supply the last branch.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-worked-example-4-mixed-diagnose-and-decide-in-ten-minutes-il\"></span>\n<h4>Worked example 4 — Mixed: diagnose and decide in ten minutes (Illustrative)</h4>\n<p><strong>Prompt (ten minutes).</strong> A software company's customer-support AI assistant shows the thumbs-down rate on answers rising from 8% to 12% over two weeks (invented). &quot;What do you do?&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Minute 0 to 2: clarify and frame.</strong> <em>Asked:</em> what counts as a thumbs-down (the share of answers rated, down, over answers rated), how many answers are rated (about 5%, invented, so selection matters), when exactly did it start (it rose gradually then stepped up around day three), and what changed recently. <em>Frame:</em> decision, whether to roll back a change or keep the system as is; question, why feedback worsened; scope, all topics, the last two weeks.</p>\n<p><strong>Minute 2 to 4: structure.</strong> Cut by what could have changed (the decision is what to roll back):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Measurement:</strong> the feedback widget, the share of people who rate, or the mix of who rates.</li>\n<li><strong>Inputs:</strong> the questions asked (a new product launch, a seasonal topic).</li>\n<li><strong>System:</strong> the assistant's model, instructions, retrieval index or knowledge base.</li>\n<li><strong>Expectations and interface:</strong> how answers are presented, or what users expect.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>&quot;I'm excluding the model provider's outage as separate; I'd treat it under system.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Minute 4 to 5.5: prioritize.</strong> The change was gradual then stepped, which suggests an event on day three. System changes in the last two weeks are the cheapest to check (a release log) and the most reversible. Question mix is cheap too (classify a sample). Measurement is a quick sanity check. I would check system and question mix first.</p>\n<p><strong>Minute 5.5 to 9: test.</strong> <em>H1:</em> a system change on day three degraded quality. Prediction: the thumbs-down rate rose across topics, not in one. <em>Reject if</em> the rise is confined to a topic with a recent product change. <em>H2:</em> the question mix shifted toward topics where the assistant is weak. Prediction: the rise is concentrated in new topics. <em>Reject if</em> the rate rose equally within unchanged topics. The interviewer provides data (invented): a knowledge-base re-index ran on day three; the rise appears across topics, including old ones; question mix is unchanged. &quot;That lowers H2 and raises H1. It also fits the step on day three.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Minute 9 to 10: conclude.</strong> &quot;I believe the re-index degraded retrieval, with medium-to-high confidence. First I would mitigate, because customers are getting worse answers now: switch to the previous index behind a flag, which is reversible and which I understand. Then I would diagnose why the new index is worse, by comparing retrieved passages for a sample of questions, and add a quality check to the re-index process. I would change my view if rolling back does not restore the rate.&quot; (The evaluation of AI products is developed in C24 and C25.)</p>\n<p><strong>What this shows.</strong> The skeleton fits ten minutes, with updates aloud as data arrives, and ends with mitigate-then-diagnose rather than a diagnosis alone.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-6-baseline-strong-standout\"></span>\n<h3>6. Baseline, Strong, Standout</h3>\n<p><strong>Prompt:</strong> &quot;Customer ratings for our food-delivery app dropped from 4.6 to 4.3 over the last two months. How would you figure out what's going on?&quot; (Numbers invented.)</p>\n<span id=\"c03-baseline-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Baseline answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;There could be a lot of reasons: late deliveries, food quality, driver behavior, app bugs, or new competitors. I'd look at customer feedback to see what people complain about most, then work on fixing that. We should also talk to the operations team.&quot;</p>\n<p><em>What is wrong:</em> a list with no structure, no clarification, no priority and no test. &quot;Look at feedback&quot; is a task, not a hypothesis, and it ends in an action with no decision behind it.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-strong-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Strong answer</h4>\n<p>&quot;First I'd clarify: is the rating the post-delivery star score, has it dropped everywhere or in some cities, and has anything changed in the last two months? Assume it's all cities and gradual. The decision is where operations should focus, so I'll cut by stage of the order: ordering, restaurant preparation, delivery, and resolution after a problem. I'll check measurement too, in case the rating prompt changed.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd start with delivery and preparation, because they're the most common drivers of low ratings and the data is available. I'd look at average delivery time and the share of late orders by city, and at ratings by restaurant type. If late orders rose in a few cities, that points to a courier-supply issue there. If ratings fell mainly for one restaurant category, preparation is the issue. I'd conclude by naming the segment that explains most of the drop and propose a fix for it.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c03-standout-answer\"></span>\n<h4>Standout answer</h4>\n<p>Everything in the Strong answer, plus:</p>\n<p>&quot;Before choosing a cut, I'd ask what a drop of 0.3 implies, because the shape of the distribution changes where I look. If 11% of orders went from 4.6 to 2.0 (an assumed example), the average falls by about 0.3 while the other 89% are unchanged. So I'd first check whether the drop comes from a small group of bad orders or a general slide, by looking at the share of one- and two-star orders. That one check steers the whole investigation.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd also test measurement and mix before operations, because they're cheap and would change the story: did the rating prompt change, did the share of customers who rate change, and did the mix of new customers shift? New customers often rate lower, so a surge in new users could lower the average without any change in service (a mix shift). The decision is where to act, so I'd then cut by order stage and look at concentrations.</p>\n<p>&quot;I'd write the first hypothesis so it can be rejected: 'Late deliveries in two cities explain most of the drop.' If late orders are flat, I reject it. If the fall is concentrated, I'd propose a mitigation that is reversible, such as courier incentives in those cities, while I look for the cause. I'd say how confident I am after each check and what would change my view.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c03-what-moves-an-answer-up-a-band\"></span>\n<h4>What moves an answer up a band</h4>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>From Baseline to Strong</th>\n<th>From Strong to Standout</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Clarifies the metric, scope and timing</td>\n<td>Reasons about what the size of the change implies</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Builds a cut tied to a decision</td>\n<td>Checks measurement and mix before operations</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prioritizes with reasons</td>\n<td>Writes hypotheses with rejection conditions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ends with a segment and a fix</td>\n<td>Proposes a reversible mitigation and states confidence and what would change the view</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c03-7-company-lens\"></span>\n<h3>7. Company lens</h3>\n<p>All claims describe what companies say publicly or what candidates commonly report, not insider knowledge. Interview formats change <code>[as of 2026-10]</code>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Google [as of 2026-10].</strong> Google's four published hiring attributes are listed in C02. Reported APM loops include product, analytical and estimation rounds where visible structure is expected <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say the cut and why you chose it, because the interviewer cannot score structure you keep in your head.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Amazon [as of 2026-10].</strong> Amazon publishes its Leadership Principles, which include Dive Deep and Insist on the Highest Standards <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Amazon's Leadership Principles page, amazon.jobs]</code>. Candidates commonly report that follow-up questions test whether you have checked a number or a claim <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: after each hypothesis, say how you would verify it and what data you would pull.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Meta [as of 2026-10].</strong> Candidates commonly report analytical (execution) rounds with metric-change questions and follow-ups on what you would measure and what the metric could hide <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: check measurement and mix shift early; interviewers often provide data and expect you to update aloud.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Microsoft [as of 2026-10].</strong> Microsoft publicly emphasizes a growth-mindset culture <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Microsoft careers culture page]</code>. Reports suggest interviewers appreciate a candidate who revises a hypothesis when data contradicts it <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: say &quot;that rules out X&quot; when it does, without defensiveness.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Apple [as of 2026-10].</strong> Third-party prep guides report team-specific loops and product-sense or technical deep-dive rounds, with structured reasoning expected inside product questions rather than as a separate drill <code>Inferred</code> <code>[unverified: candidate-reported]</code>. Practical consequence: use the skeleton inside a product answer (for example, structuring the cause of a quality complaint), and include craft and consistency among your branches.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Netflix [as of 2026-10].</strong> Netflix's published culture material describes giving people context instead of controls and expecting informed judgment <code>Public</code> <code>[V: Netflix culture memo, June 2024]</code>. Candidates commonly report prompts about content and subscription decisions in which a structure plus a way to test it is valued <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: after you structure the problem, say which branch you would check first, with what data, and what you would decide on the result.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Company lens: Stripe [as of 2026-10].</strong> Stripe is widely described as valuing clear written reasoning and precise thinking about developer and payments products <code>Inferred</code> <code>[V: The Pragmatic Engineer, Inside Stripe's Engineering Culture Part 2 (strong culture of writing); Slab interview with Stripe's former Documentation Manager]</code>. Candidates report case and product-sense prompts for payments and developer tools <code>Inferred</code>. Practical consequence: for an API or payments problem, structure by the developer funnel (sign up, integrate, go live, grow volume) and by failure type (declines, errors, latency), and write the structure down so it can be checked.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<span id=\"c03-8-common-mistakes\"></span>\n<h3>8. Common mistakes</h3>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Mistake</th>\n<th>How to detect it</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1</td>\n<td>Starting to list causes before clarifying</td>\n<td>Your first sentence is &quot;it could be...&quot;</td>\n<td>Ask the three to five questions that change your structure</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2</td>\n<td>A tree with no decision behind it</td>\n<td>You cannot say what you would do differently in branch A versus B</td>\n<td>Apply the decision test (FW-C03-02)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3</td>\n<td>Overlapping branches</td>\n<td>An item fits in two places</td>\n<td>Run the overlap test and redraw</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4</td>\n<td>A large &quot;other&quot;</td>\n<td>&quot;Other&quot; is the biggest bucket</td>\n<td>Find the missing branch</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5</td>\n<td>Depth-first on the first branch</td>\n<td>Five minutes in, only one branch is examined</td>\n<td>Set the top level first; prioritize</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6</td>\n<td>A hypothesis that cannot fail</td>\n<td>No result would change your mind</td>\n<td>Add a rejection condition</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7</td>\n<td>Never discarding</td>\n<td>You collect evidence but never say what it rules out</td>\n<td>After each check, say what it lowers and raises</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8</td>\n<td>Checking the easy thing</td>\n<td>You examine a branch because the data is handy</td>\n<td>Rank by likelihood and impact, not only cost</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9</td>\n<td>Stopping at a symptom</td>\n<td>Your &quot;cause&quot; is the metric restated</td>\n<td>Ask whether fixing it stops recurrence</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10</td>\n<td>No conclusion</td>\n<td>You end with &quot;I'd look into these&quot;</td>\n<td>Close with belief, confidence and next action</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<span id=\"c03-c03-7-failure-modes-framework-theater-overlapping-branches-f\"></span>\n<h4>C03.7 Failure modes: framework theater, overlapping branches, false completeness</h4>\n<p><strong>Framework theater.</strong> <a href=\"#gl-framework-theater\">framework theater</a> is naming or drawing a framework without using it to reach a conclusion. It looks like structure and is a substitute for it. Detect it by asking: did any step of the framework change what I said next? If the answer is no, the framework was decoration. <em>Examples:</em> reciting the four lettered steps of a memorized method with no link to the prompt; drawing a tree whose branches do not narrow anything; ending a framework with &quot;so, it depends.&quot; <em>Fix:</em> use only the steps the question needs, say why you skip the others, and tie each step to the decision.</p>\n<p><strong>Overlapping branches.</strong> The mistake in C03.2: the same item sits in two branches, so evidence can be counted twice or credited to the wrong branch. <em>Detect:</em> try to place a concrete example in the tree; if it fits two places, the tree overlaps. <em>Fix:</em> redraw along a single dimension at each level.</p>\n<p><strong>False completeness.</strong> A tree that looks exhaustive but hides a gap, often in &quot;other,&quot; in an unexamined assumption, or in a level where siblings are not comparable. It is dangerous because it signals confidence. <em>Detect:</em> ask &quot;what real cause would not fit anywhere?&quot; and &quot;what percentage sits in other?&quot; <em>Fix:</em> name what the tree excludes on purpose, and revisit the cut if the top branches yield nothing.</p>\n<p>Two more that cost time: <strong>anchoring</strong> (holding the first hypothesis too long) and <strong>the endless tree</strong> (adding levels instead of testing). The defenses are competing hypotheses and a stopping rule: stop splitting when each leaf is a check you can run.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-9-mental-models-applied\"></span>\n<h3>9. Mental models applied</h3>\n<p>Four models, each tied to a decision in this chapter. C02's models also apply here: <a href=\"#gl-first-principles-reasoning\">first-principles reasoning</a> helps separate the facts of a problem from its conventions, <a href=\"#gl-inversion\">inversion</a> sharpens a pre-mortem on your own tree, and <a href=\"#gl-sunk-cost\">sunk cost</a> is the reason to abandon a tree that is not working.</p>\n<p><strong>The Pareto principle</strong> (<code>MM-pareto</code>) says that a small share of causes usually produces a large share of effects. The <a href=\"#gl-pareto-principle\">Pareto principle</a> is why you prioritize: in Worked example 1, one of five steps held 25 of the 62 lost installs, and checking that first was most of the answer. It is a pattern, not a law: the split is often not 80 and 20, and in fraud or safety the long tail of rare events can matter more than the head.</p>\n<p><strong>Occam's razor</strong> (<code>MM-occams-razor</code>) says to prefer the simplest explanation consistent with the evidence. Application: before blaming a competitor or a market shift, check the last release and the data pipeline, which are simpler and cheaper to rule out. The limit is that complex systems often have several contributing causes, so &quot;simple first&quot; does not mean &quot;simple only.&quot;</p>\n<p><strong>Chesterton's fence</strong> (<code>MM-chestertons-fence</code>) says not to remove a rule or practice until you know why it exists. Application: a team wants to cut an apparently pointless approval step in refunds. Investigate first; the step may be a fraud control. This is the same discipline as separating fixed facts from conventions (C02.2), applied to a process you are about to change.</p>\n<p><strong>The streetlight effect</strong> (<code>MM-streetlight</code>) is searching where it is easy to look rather than where the answer is likely to be. Application: analysts spend a week in the dashboard because that is where the data lives, while the cause sits in support tickets or an offline event the dashboard does not capture. Counter it by ranking branches by likelihood and impact before cost, and by asking what data you do not have.</p>\n<span id=\"c03-engineering-design-and-data-science-perspectives\"></span>\n<h3>Engineering, design and data-science perspectives</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Engineering.</strong> Engineers often think in components and dependencies, which makes them natural fault-tree thinkers. Ask for a dependency map when a failure spans systems, and ask what changed (deploys, configuration, flags) before asking what is broken. Blameless post-incident reviews, which look for contributing conditions rather than culprits, are the engineering form of C03.8.</li>\n<li><strong>Design and research.</strong> Qualitative evidence belongs at the leaves of a tree: interviews and session recordings generate hypotheses that data then sizes, and the <a href=\"#gl-say-do-gap\">say-do gap</a> (C06) is a reason not to rely on either alone.</li>\n<li><strong>Data science.</strong> Ask what a test could not detect. A hypothesis that survives a weak test has not been confirmed. Searching many cuts until one looks significant produces false causes, which is the multiple-comparisons problem in C12.</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"c03-checklist-a-tree-before-you-commit-to-it\"></span>\n<h3>Checklist: a tree before you commit to it</h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Is the root a question with a decision attached?</li>\n<li>Does the cut serve that decision (the decision test)?</li>\n<li>Do the branches pass the overlap and gap tests?</li>\n<li>Are there three to five branches per level, at the same level of abstraction?</li>\n<li>Is &quot;other&quot; small, or have I named what I excluded?</li>\n<li>Is every leaf a check I can run?</li>\n<li>Have I scored branches and ordered the checks?</li>\n<li>Does each hypothesis have a rejection condition?</li>\n</ol>\n<span id=\"c03-10-case-studies\"></span>\n<h3>10. Case studies</h3>\n<span id=\"cs-c03-01\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C03-01 · The grocery checkout drop · Illustrative · Beginner</h5>\n<p><em>Fictional composite. Every number is invented.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> An online grocer's checkout completion fell from 71% to 59% over three weeks (invented). The product team's first proposal is to redesign the checkout page.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> Two weeks, one analyst, and a redesign would take two months.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> Step conversions (invented), before and after:</p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Checkout step</th>\n<th>Before</th>\n<th>After</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cart to address entered</td>\n<td>92%</td>\n<td>92%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Address to delivery slot chosen</td>\n<td>88%</td>\n<td>74%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slot chosen to payment</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n<td>90%</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Payment to confirmation</td>\n<td>97%</td>\n<td>97%</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Overall completion: 0.92 × 0.88 × 0.90 × 0.97 = 70.7% before and 0.92 × 0.74 × 0.90 × 0.97 = 59.4% after, matching the headline.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> The loss is concentrated at one step, delivery-slot selection, not across checkout. A redesign of the whole page does not target it.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> Hypotheses for the slot step: (H1) slots became unavailable; (H2) the slot interface has a bug; (H3) delivery fees at that step rose. Checks: the share of sessions that saw &quot;no slots available&quot; rose from 6% to 21% (invented), which supports H1; error logs showed no change in slot-page errors (rejecting H2); delivery fees were unchanged (rejecting H3). Asking why slots were unavailable led to operations, which had cut delivery windows in two zones after a driver shortage (invented), and the product team had not been told. The symptom was low checkout completion; the cause was reduced delivery capacity combined with a slot step that showed an empty state with no alternative.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong></p>\n<table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Optimizes</th>\n<th>Costs</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Redesign checkout</td>\n<td>Overall polish</td>\n<td>Two months; does not address capacity</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Restore capacity in the two zones</td>\n<td>Directly removes the cause</td>\n<td>Operations cost; depends on drivers</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Show the next available slot and offer a waitlist when none exist</td>\n<td>Recovers some lost orders now</td>\n<td>Build effort of two weeks; does not add capacity</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> B and C address different parts of the problem (supply and experience). C is reversible and fast; B costs money and takes time, and the business must decide whether the orders recovered are worth it.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> Ship C now, and bring the finding to operations with a number: each point of slot availability in those zones is worth a stated number of orders per week (invented). Drop the redesign.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> In this fictional scenario completion recovered to 66% within three weeks (invented), and operations restored part of the capacity after seeing the order-loss estimate.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> If the slot-page error rate had risen, H2 would have been confirmed and the fix would have been engineering's. If all steps had declined a little, the funnel cut would have given no concentration, and a segment cut (new versus returning, device) would have been the next try. The case shows the method: a cut that fits the decision, arithmetic that confirms the funnel, and hypotheses that can be rejected.</p>\n<span id=\"cs-c03-02\"></span>\n<h5>CS-C03-02 · Knight Capital, 2012: forty-five minutes, several causes · Verified · Advanced</h5>\n<p><em>Sources (types): the US Securities and Exchange Commission's press release of 16 October 2013 (2013-222) and its administrative order (Release No. 34-70694), both read in full, the order being the primary source for the findings; and Knight Capital Group's Form 8-K of 2013, for the company's own loss figure. The structure given to the analysis is the author's. Re-read in October 2026.</em></p>\n<p><strong>Situation.</strong> On August 1, 2012, a US trading firm, Knight Capital, deployed new software for a trading program connected to its automated order-routing system. During the first 45 minutes after the market opened, its router, SMARS, sent millions of child orders into the market while processing 212 small customer orders, producing more than 4 million executions in 154 stocks for more than 397 million shares, an unwanted net long position of about $3.5 billion in 80 stocks and a net short position of about $3.15 billion in 74 stocks, and a loss of more than $460 million, according to the SEC's order (the SEC's press release describes the router sending &quot;more than four million orders&quot;) <code>[V: SEC order, paragraphs 1 and 17; SEC press release]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Constraints.</strong> The system was trading in real time in a live market; losses grew every minute; the cause was not known at the start; and the firm could not undo trades once executed.</p>\n<p><strong>Data.</strong> From the SEC release: the figures above; that an internal system generated 97 automated emails to a group of employees before the market opened, which identified an error but were not treated as alerts and were not acted on; and that the firm agreed to pay a $12 million penalty without admitting or denying the findings. From the order itself <code>[V: SEC order, paragraphs 13 to 27]</code>: the new code was meant to replace unused Power Peg code and reused a flag that had once activated it; one of eight servers did not receive the new code because a technician did not copy it, no second technician reviewed the deployment, and there were no written procedures requiring one; on that server the flag woke the old code, which kept sending child orders because a function that counted filled shares had been moved in 2005 without the Power Peg code being retested; and during the incident Knight uninstalled the new code from the seven correct servers, which worsened the problem because more orders then activated Power Peg. Knight's own filing reported a trading loss of $457.6 million for the August 1 incident, and $468.1 million including related legal and professional costs <code>[V: Knight Form 8-K earnings exhibit, 2013]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Problem definition.</strong> At the time of the event, the symptom was unexpected, enormous order volume. The causal question was which component was sending orders and why. In hindsight, the problem was not a single bug but a set of conditions.</p>\n<p><strong>Analysis.</strong> <em>The author's structure.</em> A diagnostic tree for the first minutes might have split by where the orders originated: external (a customer, a market data fault) or internal (our router); then by which part of the router: new code, old code, or configuration; then by whether it was limited to some servers or all. The &quot;one server out of eight&quot; fact is what the tree would have isolated. In hindsight, a fault-tree view shows an AND of conditions that all had to be true: dead code still present on a production server, a flag that could activate it, a deployment that missed one server, a changed behavior in the old code that no test caught, and warnings that did not reach anyone as alarms. Remove any one and the event is smaller or does not happen. This is why searching for a single root cause misleads: the contributing causes are the story.</p>\n<p><strong>Options.</strong> <em>In the moment, as the author's reconstruction.</em> Stop the flow of orders from the router; roll back the new code; investigate before changing anything. Published accounts describe an attempt to remove the new code from the seven servers, which activated the old behavior on more of them and made the problem worse <code>[V: SEC order, paragraph 27]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Tradeoffs.</strong> Speed against understanding. A reversible action whose effects are known (halting the router's order flow) differs from an action that changes the system's behavior on a guess (removing code). The first limits the harm; the second can multiply it.</p>\n<p><strong>Decision.</strong> As documented, the firm attempted the rollback, which the order says worsened the problem <code>[V: SEC order, paragraph 27]</code>, and the loss accumulated until the problem was stopped.</p>\n<p><strong>Outcome.</strong> Documented: a loss of more than $460 million in about 45 minutes and a $12 million SEC penalty and an undertaking to retain an independent consultant to review its controls <code>[V: SEC order]</code>. The order records that Knight's parent combined with GETCO on 1 July 2013 to form KCG Holdings <code>[V: SEC order, paragraph 11]</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>Alternative outcomes.</strong> <em>The author's analysis.</em> Verifying the deployment on every server before enabling the flag would have exposed the eighth server. Removing the dead code years earlier, or using a new flag instead of repurposing an old one, would have removed the trigger. Treating the pre-open emails as alerts would have given a chance to fix the problem before trading began. A staged release to one server first, with a check on order volume, would have shown the problem in seconds. These are the practices C17 develops (<a href=\"#gl-staged-rollout\">staged rollout</a>, <a href=\"#gl-feature-flag\">feature flag</a>, <a href=\"#gl-rollback\">rollback</a>).</p>\n<p><strong>Lesson for an interview answer.</strong> Separate the symptom from the causes; expect several contributing causes; and when harm is ongoing and the cause is unknown, make the first action one you can reverse and understand. In a product interview, say it as: &quot;I'd stop the harm first with something reversible, then find the cause.&quot;</p>\n<span id=\"c03-11-interview-questions-and-model-answers\"></span>\n<h3>11. Interview questions and model answers</h3>\n<p>Ten questions: three Beginner, four Intermediate and three Hard. Model answers are <code>Illustrative</code>; numbers are invented. Each is written against the five behaviors in section 1.</p>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-01\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-01 · Beginner · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> We launched a &quot;saved searches&quot; feature and very few people use it. How would you find out why?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists reasons (&quot;people don't know about it, it's not useful&quot;) and says &quot;I'd survey users.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Clarifies what &quot;few&quot; means, structures along an adoption path, prioritizes and states what each check would show.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Separates &quot;the need is small&quot; from &quot;the need is large but the feature is not found,&quot; writes rejectable hypotheses, and ties each result to a decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> First I'd clarify. What does &quot;few&quot; mean: a share of weekly users, and against what expectation? How long since launch, and on which platforms? The decision matters too: are we deciding whether to invest further, improve how people find it, or retire it?</p>\n<p>I'll assume that 3% of weekly users have tried it against an expectation of 15% (invented), eight weeks after launch, and that the decision is whether to invest, fix discovery, or retire.</p>\n<p>I'll structure along an adoption path, because the decision is which stage to fix: do people need this (is the underlying behavior common), do they see the entry point, do they try it, does it work when they try, and do they come back. Need comes first because it decides everything else: if almost nobody repeats searches, no discovery fix will help.</p>\n<p>I'd check two things first, both cheap. Need: what share of users run the same search three or more times in a month? Say it is 25% (invented). Exposure: what share of those users ever saw the entry point? Say it is 20% (invented).</p>\n<p>Hypotheses: H1, the need is real and the entry point is not seen. Prediction: among repeat searchers, few have seen the button, and those who did try it at a decent rate. I'd reject H1 if exposure is high and the try rate is still low. H2, the feature is seen but first use fails. Prediction: many starts, few saved searches. H3, the need is narrower than assumed. Prediction: few users repeat searches.</p>\n<p>With the data I assumed, need is large and exposure is low, so H1 is supported and H3 is not. I'd test moving the entry point to the results page for 20% of users, with saved searches per active user as the metric and search satisfaction as a guardrail. I'd change my view if exposure rises and adoption stays flat, which would point to H2 and a usability problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What if exposure is high (most repeat searchers saw the button) and the try rate is low? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Then discovery is not the problem, and the hypothesis that people do not see it is rejected. I would look at the next steps on the path: the first-use experience and the value after trying. I would ask five of those who saw it and did not try it why, and check what happens to the few who tried: do they save a second search and do they return to it? Low trial after high exposure suggests the label, the promise or the effort of setting it up is wrong, so I would test a different entry point or wording before concluding the need is weak.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you decide between investing further, fixing discovery and retiring it? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;By the stage where the path breaks, which is why I structured it that way. If need is real and exposure is low, fix discovery. If need and exposure are fine but trial or return is low, improve the experience and retest. If the underlying behavior is rare (few users repeat searches), retire it and free the capacity. I would write the thresholds first, for example fix discovery if at least 25% of repeat searchers exist and under 30% have seen the entry (invented), so the choice follows the evidence and not my attachment.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-02\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-02 · Beginner · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Should a ride-hailing app add a &quot;schedule a ride in advance&quot; feature?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;Yes, many apps have it&quot; or a pros and cons list with no recommendation.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Builds a decision structure (value, feasibility, viability, risk), evaluates each, and recommends with the cost stated.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Identifies the core feasibility problem (promising availability in the future), proposes a staged test with a defined segment, and states what would change the answer.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> This is a decision question, so I'll use four branches that cover it: is the need real, can we deliver it, does it work for the business, and what is the risk and can we undo it.</p>\n<p>Value: who needs to know a ride is arranged? Typically early-morning airport trips and people with fixed appointments. I'd check what share of rides today are requested on the same day for a known time, and how often riders cancel a ride they could not guarantee. Say 8% of rides fall in this group (invented).</p>\n<p>Feasibility: the hard part is not the screen but the promise. At request time we cannot be sure a driver will be available in three days. Options include guaranteeing a driver by committing capacity, offering a best-effort reservation, or confirming a ride only an hour before. Each trades reliability for cost.</p>\n<p>Viability: a guaranteed ride costs money when supply is thin, so we need a reservation fee or a cancellation rule, and drivers need a reason to accept scheduled rides.</p>\n<p>Risk and reversibility: a failed scheduled ride is worse than a failed on-demand ride because a rider counted on it, and the damage to trust persists. But a pilot is reversible.</p>\n<p>I recommend a pilot for airport and early-morning trips in one city, with a reservation fee, giving up broad coverage to learn whether we can keep the promise. The success metric is the on-time pickup rate for scheduled rides, with driver cancellation rate as the counter-metric. I would extend only if on-time pickup stays above the rate of on-demand rides. I would stop if the fee deters most riders or the promise fails often.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> How would you decide the reservation fee and the cancellation rule for the pilot? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would start from the cost of the promise. If guaranteeing a driver means paying a standby bonus on thin-supply mornings, the fee should cover the expected cost per scheduled ride, and the cancellation rule should protect the driver who kept the slot. I would test two fee levels in the pilot with a holdout and watch conversion, cancellations and driver acceptance of scheduled rides. The fee also signals seriousness: too low and riders reserve casually and cancel; too high and nobody reserves. I would choose the level where scheduled rides are fulfilled at the target rate at a margin that is not negative.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> What would make you stop the pilot? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;I would write kill criteria in advance: if fulfillment of scheduled rides is below 95% after four weeks, or if the contribution per scheduled ride stays negative after the fee, or if the cancellation rate by drivers is above a set level (invented thresholds), I would pause. A failed scheduled ride damages trust more than a failed on-demand ride, so the fulfillment criterion is the one I would not bend. A middle result, such as 96% fulfillment but negative margin, triggers a pricing change before expansion, not a launch.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-03\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-03 · Intermediate · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> On a marketplace for handmade goods, the number of listings is growing quickly but sales per listing are falling. Structure the problem.</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> Lists possible causes (competition, quality, pricing) without noticing that the metric is a ratio.</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Decomposes the ratio, separates supply from demand, and prioritizes.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Notices the metric can fall mechanically when listings grow faster than demand, asks whether it is a problem, checks concentration, and links the finding to a decision.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I'd start by noticing that &quot;sales per listing&quot; is a ratio: total sales divided by listings. It can fall because sales fall, because listings grow faster than sales, or both. So the first question is what happened to total sales. If total sales are flat while listings grow 30% (invented), sales per listing fall about 23% mechanically (1 divided by 1.3 is 0.77), and the cause is dilution, not a deterioration.</p>\n<p>Then I'd ask whether it is a problem at all. For a marketplace, more supply is good if it serves unmet demand and bad if it spreads a fixed number of buyers more thinly and drives good sellers away. The decision is whether to limit or curate supply, improve discovery, or accept the change.</p>\n<p>I'd structure with supply, demand and matching. Demand: buyers, conversion, repeat buying. Supply: listing quality, price, distribution across sellers. Matching: how well search and recommendations connect buyers to the listings that suit them.</p>\n<p>Priorities: total sales trend (cheap, decisive), and the distribution of sales across listings (cheap): do the top 10% of listings take most sales, and is that share rising? A rising concentration says new listings are not being discovered or are weaker. Then check the quality of new listings and the match between new listings and search queries.</p>\n<p>Hypotheses: H1, new listings are weaker and buyers favor established ones; prediction: new listings' conversion is lower and concentration rises. H2, demand is flat and supply growth is dilution; prediction: total sales flat, per-listing sales of old listings unchanged. I would reject H1 if new listings convert as well as old ones.</p>\n<p>My recommendation depends on the result: if H2, focus on demand growth and avoid punishing sellers; if H1, work on discovery for new listings and on seller guidance.</p>\n<p><strong>Follow-ups.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Total sales are flat and the top 10% of listings take 80% of sales, up from 70%. What do you conclude? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;That growth in listings is mostly dilution and discovery is concentrating demand on established listings: new listings are not being found or are weaker. I would not conclude yet that supply is too high. I would check quality (photos, descriptions, prices) of the new listings, how search ranks new items, and whether buyers who purchase from the long tail repeat. The decision then is between improving discovery for new listings, curating supply by quality, or accepting concentration if the buyer experience is good.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Q.</strong> Would you ever limit the number of listings? <strong>A.</strong> &quot;Possibly, but as a last resort. A cap hurts good new sellers and sends them elsewhere. I would first improve discovery and quality signals, and use lighter tools such as requiring minimum photo and description quality, or promoting new listings for a short period to give them a fair test. I would consider limiting supply in a specific category where oversupply clearly hurts buyers and sellers, measured by buyer conversion and seller churn, and I would test it in one category before wider use.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<span id=\"iq-c03-04\"></span>\n<h5>IQ-C03-04 · Intermediate · structuring · PR</h5>\n<p><strong>Prompt.</strong> Your CEO says, &quot;Users don't love our product.&quot; How would you turn that into something you can act on?</p>\n<p><strong>Rubric.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baseline:</strong> &quot;I'd do a survey or NPS to see if that's true.&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Strong:</strong> Asks what the CEO observed, defines &quot;love&quot; as measurable behaviors, segments, and frames a question with a decision.</li>\n<li><strong>Standout:</strong> Treats the statement as a hypothesis about a segment, converts it into several measurable claims, orders the checks, and agrees on the decision with the CEO before analysis.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Model answer.</strong> I would treat this as a hypothesis from someone with a signal, and my first job is to learn what the signal was. I'd ask the CEO what prompted it: a number, a review, a conversation, or a competitor comparison. I'd also ask what decision it should drive: a roadmap change, a retention program, a repositioning.</p>\n<p>Then I'd turn &quot;love&quot; into behaviors we can measure: users return without prompting (retention and frequency), they recommend (referrals, ratings), they would be unhappy to lose it (a standard survey question such as how disappointed they would be), and they pay or upgrade. I'd also ask: which users? &quot;Users&quot; is rarely uniform; the statement may be true for one segment and false for another.</p>\n<p>So the frame becomes: &quot;Which user segments retain and recommend at lower rates than we expect, and what do they say is missing?&quot; The decision: which segment and which problem the next quarter's roadmap should address.</p>\n<p>Structure by cohort and segment: new versus returning, by use case, by acquisition source. Prioritize: retention by cohort and segment is already available, so I'd start there. Then qualitative checks on the weakest segment: ten interviews and a read of the support themes.</p>\n";
